Manual review corrections for this page's first pass.

- Every vector variable ($\text{u}$, $\text{v}$, $\text{a}$, $\text{b}$,
  $\text{c}$, $\text{i}$, $\text{j}$, $\text{k}$, $\text{w}$, $\text{q}$,
  $\text{p}$, $\text{n}$, $\text{F}$ including $\text{F}_{1}$/$\text{F}_{2}$,
  and $\text{r}$) had been converted to upright, non-bold \text{} instead of
  boldface, matching the same defect already fixed for
  vol3-2-1-vectors-in-the-plane.tex and unlike every other
  vector-notation exercise file in this repo (vol3-3-2, vol3-3-3, vol3-3-4,
  vol3-4-6, ...) and OpenStax's own boldface vector convention; changed
  every such occurrence to \mathbf{}. Units ($\text{J}$, $\text{N}$,
  $\text{m}$) and the compass-bearing letters ($\text{N}$, $\text{E}$) were
  left as \text{}, since those are typeset upright by convention, not
  vectors.
- $\text{proj}_{\text{u}}\text{v}$ and $\text{comp}_{\text{u}}\text{v}$ (the
  vector/scalar projection operators) rendered the operator name with plain
  \text{} instead of an operator macro, losing the automatic operator
  spacing around the subscript and following vector; changed to
  \operatorname{proj} and \operatorname{comp}.
- "vectors a, b, and c are given" / "vectors a and b are given" / "the angle
  ... between a and b" / "vectors a orthogonal to vector..." / "Let F be the
  resultant force..." / "the components of r are expressed..." left these
  vector variables as bare, non-mathematical prose instead of the math-mode
  form ($\mathbf{a}$ etc.) used for the same variables everywhere else in
  the file; wrapped each occurrence in math mode.
- "where x and y are nonzero real numbers" (two occurrences) left the
  component variables x and y as bare prose although the same file always
  math-wraps a bound variable introduced this way (e.g. "where $t$ is a real
  number" in sibling files); changed to $x$ and $y$.
- The methane-molecule item joined the last two points of a list into one
  math span via \,\text{and}\,, matching the pattern this skill flags
  against (\text{and} plus manual spacing instead of plain-text "and"
  between separate math spans); split into "...$R(-1,1,1)$, and
  $S(-1,-1,-1)$...".
- The same item's figure description read "There are line segments from Q
  to P, P to R and R to P" -- the third segment duplicates the second
  (reversed), leaving the triangle over P, Q, R missing its third distinct
  edge; corrected to "R to Q".
- The compass-bearing exercise had "$\text{N3}0^\circ \text{E}$": the
  digits of "30" were split across the \text{} group and the degree
  superscript's base ("N3" grouped together, then a bare "0" as the
  \circ's base), a MathML-conversion artifact that garbles "N30°E" into
  "N3" + a lone raised "0°" + "E"; corrected to \text{N}30^\circ\text{E}.
- "makes an angle of 25° with the horizontal" used a literal Unicode degree
  sign in prose, inconsistent with this same file's own convention (the two
  immediately adjacent items use $25^\circ$/$20^\circ$ for the identical
  phrasing); changed to $25^\circ$.

